# SQL Filtering Basics – Quick & Simple Guide 🚀

---

## 1️⃣ WHERE

### What it does

Filters rows based on a condition.

### Example

```sql
SELECT * FROM students
WHERE age = 10;
```

### Simple meaning

Show only students whose age is 10.

### Kid example 🧸

Like saying:

“Show only kids who are 10 years old.”

---

## 2️⃣ AND

### What it does

Checks multiple conditions together.

### Example

```sql
SELECT * FROM students
WHERE age = 10 AND marks > 75;
```

### Simple meaning

Both conditions must be true.

### Kid example 🧸

Like saying:

“Show students who are 10 years old AND scored above 75.”

---

## 3️⃣ OR

### What it does

Checks if at least one condition is true.

### Example

```sql
SELECT * FROM students
WHERE age = 10 OR marks > 85;
```

### Simple meaning

Any one condition can match.

### Kid example 🧸

Like saying:

“Show kids who are either 10 years old OR scored above 85.”

---

## 4️⃣ NOT

### What it does

Removes matching conditions.

### Example

```sql
SELECT * FROM students
WHERE NOT age = 10;
```

### Simple meaning

Show students who are NOT 10 years old.

### Kid example 🧸

Like saying:

“Show everyone except 10-year-old students.”

---

## 5️⃣ IN

### What it does

Checks multiple values easily.

### Example

```sql
SELECT * FROM students
WHERE age IN (10, 11);
```

### Simple meaning

Show students whose age is 10 or 11.

### Kid example 🧸

Like saying:

“Show kids from class 10 or 11.”

---

## 6️⃣ BETWEEN

### What it does

Filters values within a range.

### Example

```sql
SELECT * FROM students
WHERE marks BETWEEN 70 AND 90;
```

### Simple meaning

Show marks between 70 and 90.

### Kid example 🧸

Like saying:

“Show students whose marks are between 70 and 90.”

---

## 7️⃣ LIKE

### What it does

Searches for patterns in text.

### Example

```sql
SELECT * FROM students
WHERE name LIKE 'R%';
```

### Simple meaning

Show names starting with R.

### Kid example 🧸

Like saying:

“Find all kids whose names start with R.”

---

## 8️⃣ CONCAT

### What it does

Joins text together.

### Example

```sql
SELECT CONCAT(name, ' - Student') FROM students;
```

### Simple meaning

Combines words into one sentence.

### Kid example 🧸

Like joining LEGO blocks together 🧩

---

## 9️⃣ UPPER and LOWER

### What they do

Change text to uppercase or lowercase.

### Example

```sql
SELECT UPPER(name), LOWER(name)
FROM students;
```

### Simple meaning

UPPER → BIG LETTERS
LOWER → small letters

### Kid example 🧸

Like changing:
ravi → RAVI

---

## 🔟 SUBSTR

### What it does

Takes part of a word.

### Example

```sql
SELECT SUBSTR(name, 1, 3)
FROM students;
```

### Simple meaning

Gets first 3 letters.

### Kid example 🧸

RAVI → RAV

---

## 1️⃣1️⃣ IFNULL

### What it does

Replaces empty values.

### Example

```sql
SELECT IFNULL(marks, 0)
FROM students;
```

### Simple meaning

If marks are empty, show 0 instead.

### Kid example 🧸

Like filling an empty lunch box with biscuits 🍪

---

# ✅ Summary Table

| Command | Meaning               |
| ------- | --------------------- |
| WHERE   | Filter rows           |
| AND     | Both conditions true  |
| OR      | Any condition true    |
| NOT     | Opposite condition    |
| IN      | Match multiple values |
| BETWEEN | Value in range        |
| LIKE    | Pattern search        |
| CONCAT  | Join text             |
| UPPER   | Capital letters       |
| LOWER   | Small letters         |
| SUBSTR  | Get part of text      |
| IFNULL  | Replace empty values  |

---

🎯 These filtering commands help us find exactly the data we want from large datasets.

